<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/16th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Molodi</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1572">1572</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russo-crimean-wars">Russo-Crimean Wars</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of Molodi was fought in late July and early August 1572 near the village of Molodi, south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a>. A Russian army under Prince Mikhail Vorotynsky defeated the much larger army of the Crimean khan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/devlet-i-giray">Devlet I Giray</a>, which had burned Moscow the year before. The victory ended Crimean hopes of restoring <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tatars">Tatar</a> overlordship over Muscovy.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-molodi.jpg" alt="Battle of Molodi" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Wilder · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Molodi_-_v_pam%27at%27_pobedy_1572_goda_-_07.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The battle was one of the key engagements of the reign of Ivan the Terrible. While the Tsardom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> was tied down in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livonian-war">Livonian War</a>, the Crimean khan hoped to profit from the weakness of its southern borders. In the course of three expeditions <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/devlet-i-giray">Devlet I Giray</a> devastated southern Russia, defeated the Russian army and set <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> on fire in 1571.</p><p>On 26 July 1572 the Crimean army, numbering around 30,000 cavalrymen, marched towards <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> once more. The historian Spencer Tucker held that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tatars">Tatar</a> force also carried <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cannon">cannons</a>, was reinforced by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/janissaries">janissaries</a>, and badly mauled the Russian vanguard while crossing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oka-river">Oka River</a> near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serpukhov">Serpukhov</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>Prince Mikhail Vorotynsky held the supreme command of the Russian and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cossacks">Cossack</a> forces, with Prince Repnin on the left flank and Prince Odoevsky on the right. On 30 July the armies clashed near the Lopasnya River without much prior reconnaissance. The fighting went on for several days and reached its peak on 8 August.</p><p>Much of it was close melee with sabres and spears, which left the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tatars">Tatars</a>' skill in archery of little use, while the Russians used artillery and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arquebus">arquebuses</a> to great effect. Lacking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gunpowder">gunpowder</a> weapons of their own, the Tatars could not break through the fortified Russian positions, nor could they draw the Russians into a pitched battle in the open field. With a second Russian army on its way to reinforce Vorotynsky, they called off the campaign and marched back to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crimea">Crimea</a> without suffering much harm on the way.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The size of both armies is disputed. Contemporary chronicles give very large and unreliable figures for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tatars">Tatars</a>: the Novgorod Second Chronicle speaks of 120,000 men and the Moscow Chronicle of about 150,000. Modern Russian historians think the khan's army most likely numbered 40,000 to 60,000, two thirds of them the Crimean army proper and the rest Nogai, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/circassians">Circassians</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/janissaries">janissaries</a> sent by the Ottoman Sultan.</p><p>Estimates of the Russian forces range from 20,000 to 70,000 men. The Russian historian Vitaly Pensky puts the whole <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> army at 35,000 and regards the other estimates as overstated.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of Molodi” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1521 Mehmed I Giray reached the outskirts of Moscow, and in 1571 Devlet I Giray burned the city, though the next year Russian forces defeated him at the <b>Battle of Molodi</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/crimean-khanate">Crimean Khanate · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The Crimean Tatars burned Moscow in 1571, but were defeated at the <b>Battle of Molodi</b> in 1572.</blockquote><a href="/empire/tsardom-of-russia">Tsardom of Russia · Military</a></li></ul></section>
